  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Ghana Employers Association (GEA) carried out a study based on a survey to investigate the cost and underlying causes of the rising industrial disputes in the country and its effects. GEA was concerned about the rising number of complaints lodged at the National Labour Commission (NLC) and their impact on the Commission&apos;s capacity to render efficient and effective resolution to the disputes. GEA was also concerned that the Covid-19 pandemic might increase tensions and result in an increased number of disputes and collective agreement issues. To address these challenges, GEA conducted a nation-wide survey in July-September 2020 covering 441 enterprises. GEA also obtained responses from workers&apos; organizations and Labour Administration institutions. GEA presented the key findings during an online meeting gathering representatives of the Ministry of Employment and Labour Relations, the NLC, the National Tripartite Committee and workers &#x2018;organizations on October 2020. The study identified key issues that needed to be addressed, especially regarding collective bargaining, and made policy recommendations in order to secure a peaceful industrial relations environment for the Ghanaian Labour market, which provided GEA with a &quot;roadmap&quot; for its advocacy activities. In June 2021, the GEA developed consequently a position paper on good labour practices for employers and workers widely disseminated and engaged in activities to strengthen dialogue with the unions and the labour administration institutions. The GEA convened three tripartite meetings for engagement on the rising industrial disputes in the country and capacity building on dispute settlement procedures on 12, 19 and 26 August 2021 in the Ashanti, Western and Greater Accra regions attended by 115 employers, workers and unions representatives. These meetings provided GEA with an opportunity to dialogue with the government, the NLC and Ghanaian union centres for an improved dispute resolution system. The ILO supported the Ghana Employers Association (GEA) in carrying out the study on the cost and causes of rising industrial disputes in Ghana. The support entailed reviewing the methodology and design of the survey and the report. Based on this evidence-based analysis, GEA was able to take appropriate actions in order to bring about changes in attitudes from all stakeholders and foster a harmonious industrial relations environment for the Ghanaian Labour market. The ILO also provided assistance in preparing GEA position paper for good labour practices for employers and workers in Ghana with a particular focus on labour standards ratified by the country, as part of the organization&apos;s advocacy activities following the study. The ILO provided advice on the design of the document before its publication and dissemination. In addition, as recommendations of the study indicated that urgent and proactive measures were required to expose employers and workers to the standard dispute resolution mechanisms in Ghana and engage with the government, the National Labour Commission and the unions, the ILO supported the organization of three tripartite meetings in three cities including the capital Accra.</narrative>
   </description>
